  "worked_solution": "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=9,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})^{2} & =9{(x-(-\\frac{2}{3}))}^{2} \\\\  & =9{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =9({x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{4}{9}) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+12x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=9{x}^{2}+12x+4.$",
